<div class=3D"notes">
    <div class=3D"note single">
        <h1 class=3D"title">How to Work and Compound with AI</h1>

        <p class=3D"date">
            <info datetime=3D"2026-05-03 00:00:00 +0000">
                <span class=3D"no-italics">[
       =20
       =20
        <a class=3D"tag" href=3D"https://eugeneyan.com/tag/ai/">ai</a>
       =20
       =20
        <a class=3D"tag" href=3D"https://eugeneyan.com/tag/productivity/">p=
roductivity</a>
       =20
       =20
        <a class=3D"tag" href=3D"https://eugeneyan.com/tag/mechanism/">mech=
anism</a>
       =20
    ]
</span> =C2=B7 13 min read
            </info>
        </p>

        <!-- Post content -->
        <div class=3D"notebody">
            <p>How can we work effectively with AI? What=E2=80=99s the work=
flow, how does it scale, and how do we improve our systems over time? And i=
deally, it should compound. Every finished artifact=E2=80=94code, docs, ana=
lysis, decisions=E2=80=94becomes context for the next session. And each cor=
rection updates a config that reduces future errors. While I=E2=80=99m stil=
l learning, I=E2=80=99ve repeated my answers often enough that I=E2=80=99m =
writing it here so the next time I=E2=80=99m asked I can share a link inste=
ad.</p>

<p>If you use AI regularly, you likely already apply many of these practice=
s. Nonetheless, I believe the underlying principles apply broadly: provide =
good context, encode your taste as config, make verification easy, delegate=
 bigger tasks, and close the loop. If a practice does not fit, adapt the pr=
inciple and invent your own. Also notice, as you read, that none of this is=
 specific to AI. It=E2=80=99s simply how you onboard and work with any new =
collaborator.</p>

<p class=3D"image-caption">=E2=80=A2 =E2=80=A2 =E2=80=A2</p>

<h2 id=3D"context-as-infrastructure">Context as infrastructure<a class=3D"a=
nchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=
=3D"https://eugeneyan.com/writing/working-with-ai/#context-as-infrastructur=
e" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p><strong>Help models nagivate your context.</strong> For example, all my =
code lives in <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/src</c=
ode> and all my knowledge work lives in <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">~/vault</code> (organized into <code class=3D"language-pl=
aintext highlighter-rouge">projects/</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaint=
ext highlighter-rouge">notes/</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">kb/</code> and so on). When our work is organized, it makes=
 it easier for the model to retrieve context using <code class=3D"language-=
plaintext highlighter-rouge">grep</code> or <code class=3D"language-plainte=
xt highlighter-rouge">glob</code>. And by having a clean directory tree, it=
=E2=80=99s more straightforward to navigate the directory, and find and lea=
n on prior code, project docs, analysis, etc. to improve the work being don=
e.</p>

<p><strong>Connect models to your organization=E2=80=99s context.</strong> =
Models can benefit from organizational knowledge which likely lives in Slac=
k, Drive, Mail, etc. Most have <a href=3D"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/d=
ocs/getting-started/intro" target=3D"_blank">MCPs</a> for Claude Code, Cowo=
rk, Claude.ai. On top of these, I also maintain a <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">INDEX.md</code> per project. It=E2=80=99s an an=
notated index of the relevant docs and channels, and each entry includes th=
e URL, owner, and a brief paragraph explaining what=E2=80=99s inside and wh=
en to read it. The annotation helps a lot. A bare list of URLs forces the m=
odel to open every link to figure out what=E2=80=99s relevant, wasting time=
 and context. By annotating upfront, we do the heavy lifting once and store=
 it in the index.</p>

<p><strong>Onboard each new session like a new hire.</strong> With each new=
 session, the model starts with a blank slate. Thus, it helps to treat the =
per-project <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md<=
/code> like the onboarding doc we=E2=80=99d hand to a new teammate on day o=
ne. Claude scanned my per-project <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlig=
hter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code> files and highlighted that they included gloss=
aries for acronyms, project code names, and teammates with the same first n=
ame. I also have a suggested reading order in the <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code>, like telling the model to ski=
m <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">INDEX.md</code> firs=
t, then <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TODOS.md</code=
>, and finally specific topic notes.</p>

<p><strong>Build your memory layer.</strong> By default, models don=E2=80=
=99t remember what happened in the last session, so anything worth persisti=
ng should be written to disk. I split my memory layer into two buckets. <co=
de class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/vault</code> holds fact=
s such as project state, artifacts, and domain knowledge; <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/.claude</code> (along with its <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code>, <code clas=
s=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">skills/</code>, <code class=3D"l=
anguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">guides/</code>) contains my preference=
s, workflows, and personal taste. The former provides context while the lat=
ter provides configuration.</p>

<h2 id=3D"taste-as-configuration">Taste as configuration<a class=3D"anchorj=
s-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"htt=
ps://eugeneyan.com/writing/working-with-ai/#taste-as-configuration" style=
=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p><strong>Start with <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">=
~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>.</strong> Claude reads this at the start of ever=
y session. I think of it as a behavioral contract. My <code class=3D"langua=
ge-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code> contains preferences like =
how direct to be, when to push back, how to handle mistakes, what to teach =
me, etc. Here=E2=80=99s a trimmed version:</p>

<div class=3D"language-xml highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre=
 class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nt">&lt;behavior&gt;</span>
- Be direct and push back when you disagree; if my approach has problems, s=
ay so.
- When unsure about something, say you're unsure rather than guessing confi=
dently.
- When something fails, investigate the root cause before retrying.
- Keep diffs scoped to the task: no drive-by reformats or unrelated refacto=
rs.
...
<span class=3D"nt">&lt;/behavior&gt;</span>

<span class=3D"nt">&lt;teaching&gt;</span>
I'm always picking up new systems and domains. When a key term surfaces tha=
t I=20
likely haven't internalized, explain it in 1-2 sentences and then move on. =
Format:

&gt; =F0=9F=92=A1 followed by 1 - 2 sentence explanation
...
<span class=3D"nt">&lt;/teaching&gt;</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><strong>Scope it by directory: global, then repo, then project.</strong>=
 Put preferences that apply everywhere (e.g., behavior, long-term goals, te=
aching) in <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~/.claude/C=
LAUDE.md</code>. Put conventions for a specific a repo (e.g., linting, nami=
ng, pull requests) in the repo=E2=80=99s root. Put project-specific context=
 (i.e., directory layout, domain knowledge) in the project directory. When =
you start Claude Code in a subdirectory, it walks up the tree and loads eac=
h <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code>. An=
d when the model navigates into a subdirectory mid-session, the model picks=
 up that directory=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-=
rouge">CLAUDE.md</code> too. More in the <a href=3D"https://code.claude.com=
/docs/en/memory#how-claude-md-files-load">docs</a>.</p>

<p><strong>When CLAUDE.md gets too long, split it out.</strong> A long <cod=
e class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code> can becom=
e a context tax. It loads everything every session even if the session does=
n=E2=80=99t need it. To fix this, refactor chunks into guides that load laz=
ily. Don=E2=80=99t <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@im=
port</code> them (because that just inlines them). Instead, tell your <code=
 class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code> to read th=
em when relevant. This way, a session that=E2=80=99s building evals skips t=
he guide on writing docs. Here=E2=80=99s an example guide section:</p>

<div class=3D"language-xml highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre=
 class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nt">&lt;guides&gt;</span>
- Docs, 1-pagers, any writing: ~/.claude/guides/writing.md
- Eval building and reports: ~/.claude/guides/evals.md
- Dashboards: ~/.claude/guides/dashboards.md
...
<span class=3D"nt">&lt;/guides&gt;</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><strong>If you do something =E2=89=A5 once a week, make it a skill.</str=
ong> A skill is a markdown file with a name, trigger, and procedure that th=
e model loads on demand. Think of skills as workflows written in markdown. =
They can include logic. For example, my <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">/polish</code> skill looks at the artifact diff. If it pr=
oduces a metric, it runs the associated eval. If it renders in a browser, i=
t checks the output via Claude in Chrome. If neither, it runs the code and =
reads the output or error. Skills encode both the steps and the judgment of=
 which steps apply. A few I have include:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/polish</code>: =
checks for bugs, simplifies the code, verify the output (via evals, Claude =
in Chrome, or something else), iterate until no critical feedback, draft th=
e PR</li>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/write</code>: i=
nterviews me for the outline, spawn research subagents, writes the draft, g=
ives feedback via adversarial critic, iterate until no critical feedback</l=
i>
  <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/daily</code>: r=
eads my calendar, slack, PRs, yesterday=E2=80=99s log, etc and writes today=
=E2=80=99s priorities</li>
</ul>

<p>I tend to keep <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">SKIL=
L.md</code> small and focused on the workflow and routing. The knowledge, l=
ike templates and scripts, are separate files that the model reads and runs=
 only when needed, just like lazy-loaded guides.</p>

<p><strong>Bootstrap skills by doing the task once and then asking the mode=
l to make it a skill.</strong> This is how I build most skills. First, I do=
 the task once, interactively, in a normal session. Then, I ask the model t=
o turn what we just did into a skill. Next, I run the skill on the same or =
similar task. Inevitably, I=E2=80=99ll need to correct the output, which I =
do in the same session so feedback is logged in the session transcript. Fin=
ally, I ask the model to update the skill based on the corrections and feed=
back. You can also seed a skill with exapmles of the desired output. Ask th=
e model to extract the patterns, like how you organize your code, or the st=
ructure and tone of your docs.</p>

<p><strong>Refine skills via the transcript, not the file directly.</strong=
>  The first version of the skill rarely works perfect because it overfits =
the original session. This is normal. When you run it and need to update th=
e output, correct it within the session. Try not to open and edit <code cla=
ss=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">SKILL.md</code> directly. Provi=
ding feedback in the session gives the model before-and-after pairs which a=
ccumulate in the transcript=E2=80=94here=E2=80=99s what we did, here=E2=80=
=99s what I wanted, and why. Once the output is right, ask the model to mer=
ge the feedback into the skill. After a few rounds, the skill converges and=
 you barely have to edit the final output.</p>

<p><strong>Nonetheless, not every task needs this context.</strong> For bra=
instorming, exploration, and rough drafts, I enjoy using simple mode (<code=
 class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE=3D1 clau=
de</code>). Here, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAU=
DE.md</code> still loads but the agentic harness=E2=80=94hooks, skills, too=
l-heavy loops=E2=80=94doesn=E2=80=99t. This gets me closer to the model, wh=
ich is what I want when I=E2=80=99m thinking out loud rather than shipping.=
</p>

<h2 id=3D"verification-for-autonomy">Verification for autonomy<a class=3D"a=
nchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=
=3D"https://eugeneyan.com/writing/working-with-ai/#verification-for-autonom=
y" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p><strong>Shift verification left; catch errors at write time.</strong> I =
think of verification as a ladder. The bottom is cheap and deterministic; t=
he top is expensive and requires judgement. We want to address issues at th=
e lowest possible rung. Near the bottom are post-edit hooks that run <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ruff format</code>, <code cl=
ass=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ruff check --fix</code> on fil=
es the model just updated. This happens deterministically and doesn=E2=80=
=99t cost tokens. Higher on the ladder are tests, evals, LLM reviews, etc.<=
/p>

<p><strong>Make it easy for the model to verify the work.</strong> Give the=
 model feedback loops to improve its output. If the system produces a metri=
c, let the model run the eval and optimize it. If the output renders in a b=
rowser, let the model inspect it via Claude in Chrome. If neither, let the =
model run it and read the error. For example, when building Docker images, =
I let the model build, read the error, edit the Dockerfile, and rebuild. If=
 I=E2=80=99m tuning a harness, the model runs evals, reads the transcripts,=
 and fixes failures. When building a dashboard, the model checks in Chrome =
that tooltips render, labels don=E2=80=99t overlap, and the narrative match=
es the numbers.</p>

<p><strong>For long-running tasks, have models watch models.</strong> Long =
sessions can drift as errors build up. One fix is to run a secondary sessio=
n with fresh context to read the original spec and the recent turns of the =
primary session. My minimal setup uses two tmux panes, one for the primary =
dev, one for the pair programmer. Initial instructions and follow-up prompt=
s are appended to a shared file. Periodically, the pair programmer spins up=
, checks the spec against the primary=E2=80=99s recent transcript, and if s=
omething=E2=80=99s off, provides feedback to course correct.</p>

<p>We can do this in various ways. For example, the pair programmer can wat=
ch for execution drift=E2=80=94is the model doing the task right? This is l=
ocal and tactical, like ignoring an error, reporting a bad metric, or diver=
ging from the spec. There=E2=80=99s also direction drift=E2=80=94is the mod=
el doing the right task? These are bigger picture and strategic, and occur =
when the model misinterprets the original intent and spends hours building =
the wrong thing. Check for execution drift often and direction drift occasi=
onally.</p>

<h2 id=3D"scaling-via-delegation">Scaling via delegation<a class=3D"anchorj=
s-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"htt=
ps://eugeneyan.com/writing/working-with-ai/#scaling-via-delegation" style=
=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p><strong>Delegate increasingly bigger chunks of work.</strong> Sometimes,=
 we pair-program with models: short tasks, fast feedback, staying in the lo=
op. This well works for fast iterations, exploratory analysis, and prototyp=
ing. But with increasingly stronger models, we should aim to delegate bigge=
r tasks. Explain your intent, constraints, and success criteria upfront, th=
en let the model work. You can=E2=80=99t delegate what you can=E2=80=99t ve=
rify, so this requires first defining success criteria and metrics. The shi=
ft is from giving instructions, one at a time, to fleshing out plans and le=
tting the model execute them end to end:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>=E2=80=9CGiven these eval suites, build isolated containers per suite =
and smoke-test that each builds. Then, do the full run, log the eval metric=
s and transcripts, and use subagents to read the transcripts and confirm th=
e evals ran correctly. Run each eval n times for confidence intervals. Fina=
lly, generate the report, verify it follows the report guide, and slack me =
the results and report URL.=E2=80=9D</p>
</blockquote>

<p><strong>Run sessions in parallel and find the bottleneck.</strong> Deleg=
ating bigger tasks means we can run more at once. Claude says I typically r=
un three to six sessions simultaneously. The bottleneck has shifted from do=
ing the work to writing clear specs and reviewing outputs fast enough to ke=
ep the pipeline moving=E2=80=94the middle is hollowing out. If parallel ses=
sions share a repo, use git worktrees so each session gets its own checkout=
 and don=E2=80=99t overwrite each other=E2=80=99s changes.</p>

<p><strong>Make sessions easy to observe.</strong> When running multiple se=
ssions, I need to know their state and which one needs attention. On my mac=
, a stop hook plays a sound when a session finishes (example below). My tmu=
x window titles use a status emoji (=E2=8F=B3 working; =F0=9F=9F=A2 complet=
e) and a short Haiku-generated label so I know what each pane is doing. The=
 Claude Code status line shows context usage and the current mode. Together=
, the stop-hook sound signals a finished task, the tmux titles shows which =
one, and the status line provides the details.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code># Example stop hook alert
"Stop": [
    {
    "hooks": [
        {
        "type": "command",
        "command": "if command -v afplay &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; then af=
play -v 1.0 /System/Library/Sounds/Glass.aiff; else tput bel; fi"
        }
    ]
}
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><strong>You can check in even if AFK.</strong> <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">/remote-control</code> in Claude Code makes thi=
s easy. While commuting or waiting in line, I open the code tab in the Clau=
de app to see what=E2=80=99s running and what=E2=80=99s blocked, and if nee=
ded, unblock a stalled session with additional context or new instructions.=
 This keeps sessions moving instead of sitting idle for hours. Only do this=
 if there=E2=80=99s something urgent though, not when you=E2=80=99re trying=
 to be present or touch grass.</p>

<h2 id=3D"closing-the-loop">Closing the loop<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " ari=
a-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"https://eugeney=
an.com/writing/working-with-ai/#closing-the-loop" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 an=
chorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p><strong>Keep the context rich by working in the open.</strong> When we d=
o our work in shared docs, repos, and channels, it makes it easier for ever=
yone=E2=80=94including models=E2=80=94to retrieve and benefit from the cont=
ext. What we share today becomes part of the org context tomorrow. Try this=
 simple test: could a new teammate replicate your work from last week using=
 only the shared context? If yes, you=E2=80=99re contributing well to the o=
rg context; if not, that precious context is stuck in your head. I automate=
 this somewhat via instructions in my <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code> to post short updates in a worklog channel=
 whenever I finish a substantial task, with links to the artifact PR or doc=
.</p>

<p><strong>Mine your transcripts for config updates.</strong> Have the mode=
l read past session transcripts to find gaps. When I scanned ~2,500 of my p=
ast user turns, a sizable percentage contained phrases like <em>=E2=80=9Cca=
n you also=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9C</em>, <em>=E2=80=9Cdid you check=E2=80=A6=E2=
=80=9C</em>, <em>=E2=80=9Cstill wrong=E2=80=9D</em>, etc. These suggest tha=
t the model should have done something unprompted, and I should update the =
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code> or sk=
ill, or that a verification step is missing or broken. Hit counts show how =
often a correction happens and the transcripts show exactly what failed. Th=
is is why I make corrections within the session, so I can use the transcrip=
t as input for my next <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>CLAUDE.md</code> or skills update.</p>

<p><strong>Refactor and prune periodically.</strong> As configs grow, they =
can overlap or conflict with each other. As a result, if the model ignores =
a rule, it can be because another rule contradicts it. Fix this by refactor=
ing periodically. Each rule or preference should live in exactly one place =
(though critical instructions can be repeated in the main <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CLAUDE.md</code>). I also check for str=
ay directory-level <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">set=
tings.json</code> and consolidate them back into <code class=3D"language-pl=
aintext highlighter-rouge">~/.claude</code>.</p>

<p class=3D"image-caption">=E2=80=A2 =E2=80=A2 =E2=80=A2</p>

<p>While the specific setup will likely change as models get better, I thin=
k the principles will remain relevant: provide good context, encode your ta=
ste, make verification cheap, delegate more, and close the loop. What we=E2=
=80=99re doing is training a collaborator, one feedback at a time. And if y=
ou think about it, these principles apply to how we work with a human team =
too.</p>

<p>To get started, have your model read this <a href=3D"https://eugeneyan.c=
om/assets/SETUP.txt" target=3D"_blank">SETUP.txt</a> and help you apply it.=
 Also, I=E2=80=99d love to learn what practices or principles you=E2=80=99v=
e found valuable=E2=80=94please comment below or <a href=3D"https://x.com/e=
ugeneyan" target=3D"_blank">reach out!</a></p>

<p>p.s. This isn=E2=80=99t <em>just</em> about personal tooling. It=E2=80=
=99s also how you=E2=80=99d design agent harnesses, set team norms, and bui=
ld org infrastructure. Try reading it again with those layers in mind.</p>


